WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
You know the feeling you get
when you learn something new

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.420
about a health problem
you’ve been trying to reverse?

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:09.840
Maybe high blood pressure,
diabetes, or heart disease.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.560
Well, there’s nothing I like better
than bringing you the information

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.320
that will help you do just that.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.840
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.936
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:22.254 --> 00:00:25.200
Today, we discover the
health benefits of sleep.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.321
And we start with the connection
between the lack of sleep and weight gain.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.640
Population studies have
found short sleep duration

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.380
has been associated with obesity
in both children and adults.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.040
Observational studies can never
prove cause-and-effect, though.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.160
Maybe the obesity is
leading to sleep loss

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.360
instead of the
other way around.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.380
Obesity can cause arthritis,
acid reflux, and apnea,

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.300
all of which can
interfere with sleep.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.240
The relationship between
obesity and sleep apnea,

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.460
where your breathing
repeatedly stops and starts

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.920
throughout the night, may
be explained by increased

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.000
tongue fat—fat deposited
inside the base of the tongue—

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.640
that may contribute to
obstructing your airway

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:06.900
when you sleep on your back.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.920
The reverse causation
explanation of the link between

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.280
obesity and inadequate sleep
is bolstered by the finding

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.400
that weight loss interventions
can improve daytime sleepiness.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.180
Potential confounding
factors also abound.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.880
For example, people with
lower socioeconomic status

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.380
often work less desirable hours
such as rotating or overnight shifts,

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.240
or may live in
noisier neighborhoods

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.160
with poorer air
quality, more crime.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.000
The link between inadequate
sleep and obesity persists

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.040
after controlling for
these kinds of factors,

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.780
but you can’t control
for everything.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.620
You can’t know for sure if sleep
deprivation leads to weight gain

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.380
until you,...put it to the test.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.620
Have people pull an all-nighter
and they get hungrier

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:51.120
and choose larger portions.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.780
Randomize people to shave even just
a few hours of sleep off every night

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.860
and they start eating an average
of 677 calories more a day

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.200
compared to the normal
sleep control group.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.020
Although individual
responses vary widely,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.260
anywhere from eating 813
calories less per day

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.080
to as many as 1,437 calories more,

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.040
on average, studies found sleep
deprivation led people to overeat

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:17.940
by about 180 to
560 calories a day.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.980
Restrict people’s sleep and they also
start craving unhealthier choices–

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.980
more snacks, more foods
that are fatty and sugary.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.100
Stick people in a brain scanner
after staying awake all night,

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.360
or after few nights of four-hour
sleep, and their reward pathways

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.360
light up brighter in response
to high calorie foods.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.020
Sleep deprivation bumps the
levels of the chief endocannabinoid

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.500
in the body, the natural chemical
we synthesize that binds

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.740
to the same receptors as the
active ingredient in marijuana.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.920
This may help explain
the nighttime nibbling.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.000
On the calories outside of the
equation, some short sleepers

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.560
may take the extra time to exercise;

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.280
others will be so sleepy
they exercise less.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.760
The extra wakefulness may
raise calorie expenditure

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.360
up to about 100 calories a day,
but if sleep-deprived individuals

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.360
are overeating hundreds
of calories, over time

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.740
sleep deprivation may end up
putting the “wide” in wide awake.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.700
With insufficient sleep
inadvertently leading to such

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:21.720
higher calorie intake, it’s no
surprise that 4 out of 5 studies

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.360
involving as few as 2–5
nights of sleep restriction

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.460
found an increase in body weight.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:30.900
In other words, if you sleep
less, you may gain more

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.560
OK, but here’s where it gets crazy.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.360
Even if you control calorie
intake, you still lose more fat

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:40.500
when you get more sleep.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.200
Overweight subjects who
normally got between

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.800
6.5 to 8.5 hours of sleep a night
were randomized to two weeks

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.480
of either 8.5 hours of sleep
a night or 5.5 hours of sleep

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.460
on the same calorie-
controlled diet.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:56.220
Then the groups switched
and spent another two weeks

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.180
on the opposite regimen.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.080
So they spent a month living in
the lab so their diets and sleep

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.880
could be totally
controlled and monitored.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.200
Just looking at the scales, sleep
duration didn’t seem to matter,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.960
during both periods they ate
the same number of calories

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:11.400
and lost the same
amount of weight,

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.020
but most of the weight lost
while getting 8.5 hours was fat,

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.980
whereas most of the weight lost
when only getting 5.5 hours

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.840
of sleep a night
was lean body mass.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.220
Same diet, but with more sleep
they ended up losing more than

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:27.420
twice as much body fat.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:30.968
So you snooze you lose… fat!

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:32.400
In our next story,

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.520
we discover how getting just
one more hour of sleep a night

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.098
may help with weight control.

00:04:37.544 --> 00:04:39.300
I featured a study
that found that

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.080
curtailing sleep can cut your
rate of body fat loss in half,

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:46.140
while exacerbating the
loss of lean mass.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.700
To get better insight into what was
going on, researchers took fat

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.460
and muscle biopsies from people
after a night of sleep loss.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.040
In terms of genes that
were turned on and off,

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.040
molecular signatures
were discovered suggesting

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.140
muscle breakdown
and fat buildup.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:03.540
That was after an
all-nighter, though,

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:06.360
and in the weight-loss study
the sleep-restricted groups

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.460
ended up getting little
more than 5 hours a night.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.400
What about a more realistic
scenario like sleeping

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.560
just like one hour
less a night?

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.860
Overweight adults were
randomized to 8 weeks

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.260
of a calorie restricted
diet or the same diet

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.700
combined with just 5 days a week
of one hour a night less sleep.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.120
The sleep restricted group achieved
the one hour a day less sleep

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.660
on weekdays but ended up sleeping
an hour more on the weekend days.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:34.200
So overall, they just cut about
3 hours of sleep out of their week.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.860
Would just those few hours a week
make any weight loss difference?

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:42.600
On the scale, no, but in
the normal sleep group,

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.880
80% of the weight loss was fat,

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.360
whereas in the group just
missing a few hours of sleep a week

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.960
it was the opposite—
80% of the loss was lean.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.320
This shows that a few hours of
“catch up sleep” on the weekends

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:58.920
is insufficient, and may in fact
be contributing to the problem

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:03.120
based on “social jetlag” effect
I explored in a previous video.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.180
A comparable study
was designed for kids,

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.820
but the sleeping periods
only lasted a week.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.280
Eight- to eleven-year olds
were randomized to either

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:14.460
increase or decrease their time
in bed by 1.5 hours per night

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.980
for a week and then
switch the following week.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:21.660
They ate an average of 134 calories
more on the days they slept less

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.760
and gained in that
week about half a pound

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:25.260
compared to the sleep-more week.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:29.460
The question then becomes would
sleeping more facilitate weight loss?

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:33.000
When it comes to body fat,
can we just, sleep it off?

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.720
The benefit of interventional
studies is that you can demonstrate

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.840
cause and effect, but
observational studies can allow you

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.700
to more easily track
people and their behaviors

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.320
over a longer time span.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.720
For example, researchers
followed a group of

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:48.900
mostly overweight individuals
who started out to averaging

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:51.540
less than six hours of sleep
a night for more than five years.

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:54.900
During that time, about
half maintained that schedule

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:57.060
but the other half increased
their sleep duration up to

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:00.600
seven or eight hours a night and
ended up gaining 5 pounds less fat.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:05.340
A study entitled “Sleeping habits
predict the magnitude of fat loss”

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:06.360
(among those cutting calories)

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:08.940
found that every
extra hour of sleep

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:11.700
at night was associated with
an extra 1-and-a-half pounds

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.460
of weight loss over a period
of about 3 to 6 months.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:18.240
That’s not the same as randomizing
people to extra sleep, though.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.640
Maybe they were sleeping more
because they were exercising more

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.680
and that’s the real reason
they lost more weight?

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:27.360
Getting people to bump their
sleep from about 5.5 hours up to 7

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.780
can lead to an overall decrease
in appetite within two weeks,

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:32.940
particularly for sugary
and salty foods.

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:36.240
A four-week study getting
habitually short sleepers to sleep

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.220
about an extra hour a night
led them to consume about

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:41.160
two fewer spoonful’s worth
of sugar a day compared to

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.080
the control group but
this didn’t translate

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:45.180
into any changes in
body composition.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.340
A twelve-week study,
on the other hand,

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.320
randomizing overweight
and obese individuals

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:52.380
to a weight loss intervention
with or without a sleep component

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:56.280
found that the sleep group lost
weight significantly faster.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:01.200
A national cross-sectional
survey suggested lower obesity rates

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.720
among kids in households that
regularly ate dinner together

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:06.720
as a family, got adequate
sleep, and limited screen times,

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.960
and so Harvard researchers
decided to try to...

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:11.940
put those behaviors
to the test.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:15.720
A six-month randomized trial
to improve household routines

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:20.460
for obesity prevention among young
children resulted in a lower BMI.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.160
Normally it’s hard to
tease out the effects

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:26.220
of multi-component
interventions, but in this case

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:30.060
exhortations to limit overall
TV watching didn’t work,

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:33.360
and the families were already
eating together 6 days a week

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:34.980
and so that didn’t
change much either.

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:37.020
The only thing they
were able to get the kids

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:39.420
to significantly alter
was their sleep,

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:42.660
and so the improved weight
outcomes may be attributed

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:47.820
at least in part to the ¾ hour
average increase in nightly sleep.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:51.960
Overall, most sleep
improvement interventions

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:53.940
tended to show
improved weight loss.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:57.780
I was intrigued to look up
the one study that didn’t.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:00.420
The nice thing about
systematic reviews

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:02.940
(as opposed to so-called
“narrative” reviews)

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:06.720
is that they exhaustively
include mention of every study

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.820
that meets some
prespecified criteria.

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:11.400
This keeps reviewers
from cherry-picking,

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:14.760
but it can also lead to the
inclusion of some strange studies.

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:19.380
In this case, a randomized controlled
trial of didgeridoo playing,

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.720
the indigenous Australian
wind instrument.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:25.860
Those randomized to the didgeridoo
to improve their sleep quality

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:27.240
did not lose any weight,

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:30.900
but they also failed to improve
the quality of their sleep

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:34.636
(or, likely, that
of their neighbors).

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:40.560
Did you know that lactucin,
the hypnotic component of lettuce,

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.200
is a natural dietary
remedy for insomnia?

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.202
Here’s the story.

00:09:45.915 --> 00:09:49.800
There is a perception that time
spent asleep is time wasted,

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:53.340
but it is widely recognized that
inadequate sleep is associated

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:55.800
with multiple acute
and chronic conditions,

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:58.560
and results in the increased
risk of death and disease.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:02.760
Force people to go one week with
only six hours of sleep a night,

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:06.120
and you can change expression
of more than 700 genes.

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:09.960
The most dire effect may
be endothelial dysfunction.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:14.400
The endothelium is the thin layer of
cells that covers the internal surface

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.580
of blood vessels and is responsible
for allowing our arteries

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.280
to relax and dilate
back open properly.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:24.480
Randomize people for about a week
to get five rather than seven hours

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:27.660
of sleep, and just that two-hour
difference a night resulted

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.720
in a significant impairment
in artery function.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:36.240
Okay, but what do these numbers mean?
How bad is a week of 5-hour nights?

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.880
Sleep deprivation is no joke.

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:43.140
The magnitude of impairment is similar
to that reported in people who smoke,

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.500
have diabetes, or have
coronary artery disease.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:50.160
No wonder people who sleep less than
seven hours a night may experience

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:53.340
a 12% to 35% increased
risk of premature death

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:55.500
compared to those who
get a full seven hours.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:58.740
Yet a significant proportion
of the population may

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.140
routinely get less than that.

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:04.560
Sufficiently long, restful
sleep sessions each night

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:08.760
are said to be an indisputable
cornerstone of good health.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:11.100
Okay, so what can we do about it?

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:14.760
Those who have sleep apnea,
a common consequence of obesity

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:18.600
that interferes with sleep, benefit
from the use of CPAP machines

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:21.900
while they're losing the weight to treat
the underlying cause, hopefully.

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:24.780
But what if apnea isn't your problem?

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:28.680
What if you just have trouble
falling asleep or staying asleep?

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:33.060
In my book How Not to Diet, I have a
whole section on sleep enhancement,

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:35.370
where I go through the Four
Rules of Sleep Conditioning

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:37.500
and the Four Rules
of Sleep Hygiene.

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:41.280
What if you follow those guidelines
but still can't get to sleep?

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:46.140
Any natural dietary remedies?
I already have videos on using

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:49.680
kiwifruit to fight insomnia,
and tart cherries, too.

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:52.200
Are there any vegetables
that might help?

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:57.180
Lactuca sativa is a plant that
has been traditionally used

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:58.560
in the treatment of insomnia.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:03.480
What is this exotic sounding
leafy vegetable? Lettuce!

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:08.040
Evidently lettuce extracts have been
used from the time of the Roman Empire

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:11.940
as agents with sedative and
sleep-inducing properties.

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:16.560
Lettuce actually does have a hypnotic
substance in it called lactucin,

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:19.260
which is what makes
lettuce taste a little bitter.

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:23.340
But you don't know if it actually
works until you put it to the test.

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:29.280
And it works...in toads.
But it also works in rodents.

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:32.700
Sleep in both mice and rats
is enhanced by romaine lettuce.

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:36.120
They used romaine since
it has a higher lactucin content

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:39.720
compared to other lettuces.
Okay, but does it work in people?

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:43.860
About 10 years ago, a study was
published in which insomnia sufferers

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:48.600
were randomized to receive lettuce seed
oil, oil extracted from lettuce seeds.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:52.260
Within a week, about 70% of those
in the lettuce seed oil group

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.720
said their insomnia very much
or much improved,

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:57.120
compared to only 20% in
the placebo control group.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:01.320
The researchers concluded that lettuce
seed oil was found to be a useful,

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:05.640
safe sleeping aid in geriatric patients
suffering from sleeping difficulties.

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:09.960
They chose to study older individuals
because insomnia affects surprisingly

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:13.920
20 to 40% of older adults
at least a few nights a month.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:18.360
You think that's bad. Sleep
disturbances can plague as many

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:21.240
as nearly 8 out of 10 women
during pregnancy.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.480
Of course, there's lots
of different sleeping pills,

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:27.000
but they may endanger
the fetus or mother.

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:31.020
For example, doctors frequently
prescribe Ambien for pregnant women

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:34.380
who have trouble sleeping, but Ambien
use is associated with a wide range

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:36.060
of adverse pregnancy outcomes

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:40.080
like low birth weight babies,
premature birth, and cesarean section.

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:43.020
And the use of valium during
pregnancy has been linked

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:46.020
to birth defects
including limb deficiencies.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:52.440
There has to be a better way.
What about trying lettuce?

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.620
The lettuce oil study had
a number of limitations.

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:59.040
For example, it was only single-blind,
meaning the researchers knew

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.260
who was on the lettuce supplements
and who was on placebo,

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:05.100
which could have introduced some bias.
But the researchers essentially said,

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:09.060
"Give us a break. Big pharma
has billions to spend on research.

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:11.340
No one wants to fund
studies on lettuce."

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:15.300
Finally, we got a double-blind,
placebo-controlled study,

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:19.020
but this time on a whole food,
not just a lettuce seed extract.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:22.860
Yeah, but how do you come
up with a placebo lettuce?

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:24.960
How are you going to hide who
gets lettuce and who doesn't?

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:28.320
Well, you can't fit a head
of lettuce into a capsule,

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:31.800
but you can fit whole lettuce seeds.

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:35.880
And here we go: a double-blind
randomized placebo-controlled trial

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:39.360
on lettuce seeds for
pregnancy-related insomnia.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:42.540
A hundred pregnant women
with insomnia were randomized

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:45.600
to receive capsules containing
either a quarter teaspoon

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:48.420
of ground lettuce seeds or
a placebo for two weeks,

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.900
and those on the lettuce seeds
saw a significant improvement

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:53.580
in a sleep quality index score

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:57.594
compared to placebo
with no reported side effects.

